// core/filesystem is the small hosted path and directory boundary used by
// compiler and interactive tools. It resolves one existing UTF-8 path to a
// caller-owned canonical absolute spelling, and enumerates an opened directory
// through one copied entry name at a time. The caller owns all output storage
// and must close every successfully opened Directory exactly once.
//
// Canonical paths follow symlinks, use `/` separators, and have no trailing
// separator except a filesystem root. Enumeration order is deliberately
// unspecified because native filesystems do not provide a semantic order. A
// caller whose result is user-visible or enters a manifest/hash must sort the
// copied names explicitly. Target-selected files own native handle, path,
// layout, and encoding details. This common file owns only the portable value
// vocabulary. Draft specification: sections 3, 5-7, and 12, plus the selected
// target profile.
package filesystem

import core/io

// Entry_Kind is a best-effort native classification. `unknown` includes
// symlinks and filesystem objects whose type the enumeration API does not
// expose. A caller that wants to enter an `other` row as a directory may try
// opening that complete path; classification is not an authorization check.
pub Entry_Kind :: enum u8 {
    unknown,
    regular_file,
    directory,
}

// Entry describes the prefix initialized in the destination passed to read.
// name_length is a byte count, excludes any terminator, and is valid only when
// read returns io.Error.none. Names are UTF-8 on every hosted target.
pub Entry :: struct {
    kind: Entry_Kind,
    name_length: usize,
}
